      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e605fe81-8ae9-5f81-9f29-4e2e03aeb1d5",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x^{3} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x^{3}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x^{3} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\int \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\int \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(x^{3} \\right)}}{3}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
