        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 3 x \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x^{2}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2 x.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 3 x \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{3 \\tan{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 \\tan{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}}{2}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
